Project Overview

Full-stack web application for speedcubing training and analysis.
It includes:

A frontend (user interface) built in modern web tech.

A backend that handles cube solving, training stats, and user data.

A database for persistent storage of solves, settings, and progress.

Tech Stack Summary
Layer	Tool / Framework	Language	Purpose
Frontend (Presentation Layer)	Vue.js	JavaScript / TypeScript	Framework for building an interactive, component-based user interface (timer, stats, cube visualiser, etc.). Handles all user interactions and sends data to the Flask backend.
	HTML / CSS	—	Structure and styling of the website. CSS defines the layout and design; HTML provides the basic page framework.
	Chart.js or Recharts (optional)	JavaScript	Used to visualise training data (e.g. averages, progress charts, regression predictions).
	three.js (optional)	JavaScript	For 3D cube visualisation (rotating cube, showing solver steps).
Backend (Logic Layer)	Flask	Python	Lightweight web framework that connects your backend logic (solvers, statistics, etc.) to the frontend. It exposes API endpoints like /api/add_time or /api/solve_cube.
	Your Python modules (e.g. Solver, Cube, Training)	Python	Contain the core logic — A* algorithm, regression analysis, cube validation, etc. Flask will import and use these internally.
Database (Data Layer)	SQLite / MySQL	SQL	Stores user info, solves, training data, averages, and predictions. Flask connects to this via SQL queries or SQLAlchemy.
Machine Learning / Algorithms	—	Python	Implements algorithms such as A* for cube solving and Linear Regression for performance prediction.
Version Control & Deployment (optional)	Git / GitHub	—	For version tracking, documentation, and project backups. (You can include this in your evaluation for maintainability marks.)

How They Work Together
1. Frontend (Vue.js + JS)

User interacts with timer, visualiser, and training interface.

Sends data (solve times, cube states) to Flask using HTTP requests.

Displays responses (like stats or solutions) dynamically.

2. Backend (Flask + Python Modules)

Flask receives requests from Vue.

Passes data to your backend classes — e.g. A* solver, statistics, or learning module.

Returns the processed results (as JSON) back to the frontend.

3. Database (SQL)

Flask stores and retrieves data via SQL.

Handles persistent information such as:

Users and authentication details

Solves (scramble + time + date)

Training progress and stats